Spillway Strut
Ingredients: DUBSTEP + SOUL JAZZ / JAZZ-FUNK + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Cymbals
Creative Catalyst: Born in the summer heat at an unlicensed solar-rigged rooftop party fifty stories above the sunken canals of flooded New Mumbai, 2091.

Spillway Strut is a riotous, swaggering collision of mid-century jazz-funk joy and catastrophic half-time sound-system pressure. It transplants the warm, swinging strut of Hammond organs, punchy gospel brass stabs, and dazzling acoustic cymbal chatter straight into the chest-caving, 140 BPM sub-bass drops and vowel-churning wobbles of classic dubstep. Rather than dystopian grit, the sound is intoxicatingly jubilant—an open-air, roof-shaking carnival of defiance that treats tidal ruins like a dancefloor.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 138–142 BPM. The groove locks into a swinging dubstep half-time displacement, but the grid is deliberately humanized by syncopated soul-jazz shuffles, dragging snare ghosts, and frenzied triplet cymbal pushes.
- Harmonic Palette: Rich dominant 13th chords, blues-scale saxophone runs, and soaring major-pentatonic gospel turnarounds. The tracks constantly resolve from ominous low-end tension into triumphant, sunlit Mixolydian releases.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Acoustic Cymbal Hybridation: Vintage, riveted sizzle cymbals and raw china splashes are close-mic’d through analog preamps, layered with tape saturation, and sidechained violently to wobble LFOs so the brassy decay dances across the drop.
  2. Organ-Modulated Resampling: Warm Leslie-cabinet Hammond chords are chopped and routed through formant filters, creating vocal-like "talking" mid-range riffs that duel with live brass.
  3. Tidal Sub Weight: A completely clean, earth-quaking 30–50 Hz sine sub-bass foundation that underpins frantic live acoustic drums without muddying the horn arrangements.
- Dynamics: Songs construct deep, organic tension through intimate acoustic trio breakdowns—brush work, walking upright bass, and smoky Rhodes chords—before erupting into explosive, confetti-cannon drops where monstrous bass wobbles mirror the melodic hooks played by a roaring live horn section.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre crystallized during the monsoon surges of 2091 across the archipelago towers of submerged New Mumbai. With municipal authorities abandoning the lower thirty floors to rising sea tides, the city’s youth claimed the high-altitude rooftop utility decks. Hauling diesel generators, salvaged brass horns, and bespoke bass cabinets up fire ladders, crews linked solar batteries to bypass grid surveillance. Dancers developed a distinctive, light-footed skanking stride dubbed the "tide-hop," wearing waterproof pond-silk tunics, salt-corroded jewelry, and bright iridescent beetle brooches. The music wasn’t an act of mourning; it was an unapologetic assertion of life, where the clash of drum cymbals and tectonic bass frequencies physically scattered the pelicans and patrol skiffs below.

Central Instruments
- Acoustic Cymbals: 22-inch Constantinople ride cymbals, riveted sizzle crashes, and hand-hammered splash stacks (essential for the signature top-end sparkle and rhythmic swing).
- Hammond B3 Organ & Leslie Speaker Cabinet: Delivering grease-laden, syncopated jazz-funk comping and gospel swells.
- Tenor Saxophone & Flugelhorn: Horn sections providing celebratory call-and-response riffs.
- Analog Sub-Bass Synthesizers: Heavy Moog-style oscillators generating seismic 140 BPM drops.
- Fender Rhodes Mark I: Modulated with vintage phasers and light tape flutter.

